// EXPECT: E0262 — `#insert` with no string-literal operand (ADR-0072 §5)
//
// **Filed here rather than under `type-errors/`, and the rule is the stage rather than the imports.**
// That directory's harness asserts its files "parse, lower and resolve cleanly" and then report exactly
// their declared code *from `jr-sema`*. E0262 is a **lowering** diagnostic, so a file expecting it fails
// the first half of that contract before the second is ever reached — which is exactly what happened when
// this file was first written into `type-errors/`: two tests failed, one saying the file did not lower
// cleanly and one saying it reported nothing. ADR-0050's three `using` refusals (E0250) are in this
// directory for the same reason, and this file importing nothing is what that precedent already permits.
//
// `#insert` takes a **string literal** of Jairs source and nothing else. This wave deliberately stops
// there, and the reason is a dependency *direction* rather than difficulty.
//
// A computed operand — `#insert build_it();` — is the feature W4 §2.1 really means. Lowering produces the
// HIR that `resolved` consumes, which `checked` consumes, which `file_consts` consumes; so an `#insert`
// asking for a computed value would need `file_hir` to depend on `file_consts`, closing
//
//     file_hir → file_consts → checked → resolved → file_hir
//
// which is a salsa cycle. It is the same shape ADR-0069 §1 hit and had to restructure around, and it rests
// on the same fact ADR-0018 §3 established and both ADR-0070 §1 and ADR-0071 §2 relied on: const-eval runs
// *downstream* of the front end. Breaking it is its own sub-wave, and PLAN §5 calls it the project's top
// risk.
//
// **A named constant is refused too**, even though its value is a string the signature phase may already
// know — because accepting the easy case would make the refusal depend on how the string was written,
// which is a rule nobody could predict from the error message. `#insert CODE;` where `CODE :: "x := 1;"`
// therefore fails as well; it is not the case shown here only because it *also* fails to parse (the
// parser expects a `;` after `#insert`, so a bare name produces an E0100 first), and this directory's
// contract is one diagnostic per file.
//
// **Why the bare form is what this file shows.** `#insert;` is the one non-literal spelling that reaches
// lowering cleanly and produces exactly one diagnostic. It is also the honest minimum: the refusal is
// about the *absence of a literal*, not about any particular thing written in its place.
//
// The message names the reason rather than only the rule, because "needs a string literal" alone would
// read as an arbitrary restriction — a reader who wrote `#insert build_it()` deserves to know that the
// evaluator runs after lowering, which is a fact about the compiler they can act on by inlining the text.
main :: () {
    // E0262: no string literal to parse.
    #insert;
}
